  "text": "%%0%% is the discipline of setting each stage's level so the signal stays far above the %%1%% below and far below the %%2%% above at every link.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "gain staging"
    ],
    [
      "noise floor"
    ],
    [
      "clipping ceiling"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "One is the discipline; the other two are the enemies waiting at every link."
};

const rfHotSpot = {
  "prompt": "A producer's plan for tracking the album contains one faulty line — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Set gain during a confident rehearsal pass at performance level.",
    "Tape saturated gracefully — pushing levels added a pleasing compression.",
    "Digital saturates the same way, so hot levels flatter the file too.",
    "Modern practice records conservatively and adds character in the mix on purpose."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Digital has no sweet spot at the top — 0 dBFS is a cliff, not a colour; the same habit that flattered tape writes irreversible distortion into a file.",
  "why": "Tape flattered hot levels; the converter slices them — record conservatively, then lift the quiet, healthy file later."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/music-technology/foundational/recording-fundamentals">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Music Technology. You need nothing from earlier lessons —
    only ears and patience. Everything the discipline calls "fundamentals" is built here,
    step by step, and it is deliberately unglamorous: levels, placements and habits, not
    plugins.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace the signal chain from voice to file — source, microphone, preamp, converter — and say what each link does;</li>
    <li>choose between dynamic and condenser microphones, and explain why the choice depends on the source and the room;</li>
    <li>place a microphone using the three levers — distance, axis and spacing — and explain why placement beats EQ;</li>
    <li>set gain at every stage so the signal stays clear of the noise floor below and the clipping ceiling above, with real numbers;</li>
    <li>run a session that protects the performance: comfort, monitoring, whole takes, and honest notes;</li>
    <li>judge your playback system and calibrate your ears with reference tracks before trusting any decision.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: capture, don't rescue. Every technique on this page exists because a decision
    made before pressing record is worth more than any correction applied after.
  </p>

  <h2>A chain of translators</h2>
  <p>
    Recording is the art of converting a performance into a signal — cleanly, at the right
    level, with every choice made deliberately. It helps to picture the studio as a chain of
    translators: a voice or drum makes pressure waves in air; a microphone turns those waves
    into a tiny electrical voltage; a preamp raises that voltage to a workable level; a
    converter turns the voltage into numbers a computer can store. At every link, two enemies
    wait: <strong>noise</strong> rising from below and <strong>distortion</strong> dropping
    from above. Recording fundamentals is the craft of getting the music between them —
    every time, on every take, without panic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the chain from voice to file" widget="order" config={rfChainOrder} />
  <p>
    Why does this matter so much? Because a recording is made <em>once</em> and mixed many
    times. Editing, tuning and mixing are powerful, but they only reshape what exists:
    digital tools can fix timing and pitch, yet a badly recorded source is a permanent tax —
    a vocal clipped at the converter cannot be un-clipped, a roomy thin guitar sound cannot
    lose its room. The discipline's oldest maxim is "capture, don't rescue": decisions made
    before pressing record are worth more than any plugin applied after.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="you hear the clip — decide what happens next" widget="branch" config={rfClipBranch} />
  <p>
    The fundamentals divide into three connected skills: choosing and placing microphones
    (which translator do you use, and where do you point it?), signal flow and gain staging
    (how loud is the signal at each link?), and capturing a performance (the human side —
    takes, monitoring, and the room itself). Everything on this page serves one test: does
    the raw recording sound like the source, at a healthy level, with no noise or clipping
    baked in?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the signal chain and its two enemies" widget="equationStep" config={signalCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are ordered by causality, not by
    equipment. Nothing downstream of the source can invent what the source never made, and
    nothing downstream of a bad placement fully undoes it — which is why the chain is a
    checklist to work through in order, front to back, before any problem is blamed on
    later, more expensive links. Notice also step 5: it is not an audio step at all, but the
    attitude that governs the other four.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the studio is a chain of translators with two enemies — noise
    below, distortion above. First link: choosing and aiming the translator.
  </p>

  <h2>Microphones and placement</h2>
  <p>
    Microphones fall into two great families. <strong>Dynamic</strong> microphones (the
    Shure SM57/SM58 family is the classic) work like a tiny loudspeaker in reverse: sound
    moves a diaphragm attached to a coil in a magnetic field, generating voltage. They are
    rugged, cheap-ish, tolerant of brutal sound-pressure levels and awkward rooms — which is
    why they live on guitar amplifiers, snare drums and stages. <strong>Condenser</strong>
    microphones instead measure the distance between a charged diaphragm and a backplate,
    giving them far finer detail and sensitivity — the large-diaphragm condenser is the
    default studio vocal mic — but they need +48 V <em>phantom power</em> down the cable,
    they hear everything (including bad rooms and computer fans), and they overload if you
    park one in front of a kick drum.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="drag the divider — the two great microphone families" widget="compare" config={rfMicCompare} />
  <p>
    Placement is the cheaper and mightier tool, and it works through three levers.
    <strong>Distance</strong> sets the direct-to-room blend: a vocal mic 2–5 cm from the
    lips is almost all direct sound, intimate and dry; pulled back to 30 cm it breathes with
    the room. <strong>Axis</strong> sets tone: most mics are brightest on-axis, so singing
    slightly off-axis tames harshness without EQ. <strong>The 3-to-1 rule</strong> governs
    multi-miking: when two mics capture the same source, keep their spacing at least three
    times the distance to the source, or the delay between them comb-filters the blend. Add
    <strong>proximity effect</strong> — directional mics boost bass as they get closer,
    which makes thin voices warm and boomy voices worse — and you can see why experienced
    engineers change the mic's position before reaching for EQ.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each move under the lever that does the work" widget="sort-buckets" config={rfLeverBuckets} />
  <p>
    A worked case: recording a guitar amplifier. The standard move is a dynamic (SM57) a
    hand-width off the grille, aimed where the cone meets the dust cap — bright and present.
    Slide it towards the centre and the tone hardens; towards the edge of the cone and it
    darkens; pull it back a foot and the room arrives. The engineer's routine is to move the
    mic through those positions while the player repeats a loop, listening for the one where
    the tone is right at the source — because at the source, correction is free.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each mic position with the tone it returns" widget="match" config={rfAmpMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> expensive microphones make good recordings. It feels
  true because price must buy <em>something</em> — and it does: detail, consistency, lower
  self-noise. But a well-placed SM57 in a good position beats a boutique condenser badly
  placed every time; placement and the room dominate price in almost every practical
  test. The microphone is the last few percent; the position is the first fifty.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — price versus position" widget="judge" config={rfPriceJudge} />

  <h2>Signal flow and gain staging</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Signal flow</strong> is the map of a studio: source → microphone → preamp →
    converter → recorder, and back out through playback. <strong>Gain staging</strong> is
    the discipline of setting each stage's level so the signal stays far above the noise
    floor and far below the clipping ceiling at every link. Think of it like water pressure
    through a series of pipes: each stage can be turned up, but the whole chain only works
    if the level is right at each joint.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the blanks — the discipline and its two bookends" widget="cloze" config={rfGainCloze} />
  <p>
    The two bookend failures are easy to state. <strong>Noise</strong>: if the signal is too
    quiet, the preamp's own hiss and the room's hum sit audibly underneath it, and no amount
    of later volume-raising separates them — you amplify the noise with the music.
    <strong>Clipping</strong>: if the signal overdrives a stage — especially the digital
    converter, which has an absolute ceiling (0 dBFS) — the waveform's tips are sliced off,
    and the harsh distortion is written permanently into the file. Digital recording
    concentrates the anxiety: there is no tape compression forgiving hot levels.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="signal, noise and headroom down the chain" widget="sliderLab" config={gainCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> run the numbers. A singer produces a source level
    around 60 dB at the mic; with 30 dB of preamp gain the signal lands at 90 dB against a
    15 dB noise floor — about 75 dB of signal-to-noise, comfortable — with roughly 10 dB of
    headroom before the ceiling. Now raise the preamp to 45 dB: signal-to-noise improves to
    90 dB, but headroom shrinks to under 5 dB, and one passionate chorus clips the take.
    The two readouts are a trade-off, not a pair of maxima — gain staging is the search for
    the middle, not the top.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional habit falls out of the panel: set gain during a confident rehearsal
    pass at performance level, then leave roughly 12–18 dB of headroom. Quiet, healthy
    digital files cost nothing to lift later, but clipped ones cost everything.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> record as hot as possible like the tape era taught. It
  seems reasonable because tape saturated gracefully — pushing levels added a pleasing
  compression. Digital has no sweet spot at the top — 0 dBFS is a cliff, not a colour —
  and the same habit that flattered tape writes irreversible distortion into a file.
  Modern practice records at conservative levels and adds character in the mix on
  purpose.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the faulty line — the tape-era habit" widget="spot-error" config={rfHotSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the source is chosen, placed and gained — a clean, healthy
    signal at every link. The remaining skill is the one no meter shows: keeping the human
    performance alive while it happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Capturing a performance</h2>
  <p>
    The room is a band member. Every recording includes the space it happened in:
    reflections arrive milliseconds after the direct sound and define its character.
    Bedrooms are famously boxy because small rooms stack early reflections into comb
    filtering — a hollow, phasey sound that follows the take forever. Treated rooms and
    close-miking tame them; duvets, mattresses and closets are the honest budget versions.
    You can add artificial room later with reverb, but removing a bad room is one of audio's
    impossible tasks — which is why the discipline teaches clapping in the space and
    listening before pressing record.
  </p>
  <p>
    Capturing also means protecting the <em>performance</em>. Recording is a psychological
    situation: the engineer's first jobs are to make the player comfortable, to monitor
    (headphones) well enough that the artist can hear themselves, and to manage takes
    without breaking concentration. Practical protocol: dial in sounds before the artist
    arrives; record whole passes rather than fragments so musical flow survives; mark
    keeper takes live; comp (assemble) the best moments afterwards rather than punching in
    obsessively. Many great records were made on modest equipment with excellent
    performances — the reverse almost never happens.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the mix can fix anything. It is a comforting belief
  because modern mixing tools look miraculous — and they are, within limits. Mixing
  reshapes balance, space and tone within what was captured; it cannot invent a take's
  energy, restore clipped transients, or remove a room it was baked into. Contrast two
  recordings of the same song: the well-captured one mixes in an afternoon; the badly
  captured one fights every fader forever. The recording IS the production.</p>

  <h2>Monitoring and the control room</h2>
  <p>
    Recording decisions are only as good as the monitoring they are made on: every choice —
    mic position, gain, the take itself — is judged through speakers or headphones, so the
    playback chain is as fundamental as the capture chain. <strong>Studio monitors</strong>
    (nearfield speakers placed at listening height, forming a triangle with the engineer's
    ears) are designed for flat, honest response — not to flatter the sound but to reveal
    its problems. <strong>Headphones</strong> complement them for detail work and for
    isolating what the artist needs to hear, at the cost of a slightly distorted sense of
    space. The practical literacy is <em>reference</em>: professionals play known, loved
    recordings through the same system to calibrate their ears before judging new material,
    because ears adapt to whatever they have been hearing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The room shapes monitoring the way it shapes capture. Speakers interact with surfaces:
    reflections smear stereo imaging, bass builds in corners and cancels at boundaries, and
    an untreated bedroom "mixes" a bass-heavy fiction that collapses on other systems.
    Budget studios fight back with the same physics in reverse — first-reflection panels,
    bass traps, the equilateral-triangle setup, and the humbling habit of checking mixes on
    earbuds, a laptop, and a car before calling them done. Capture and monitoring are one
    loop: you record through microphones, decide through speakers, and the discipline is
    keeping both links of the chain honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You must record a whispered vocal in a roomy bedroom. Dynamic or condenser, close or distant — and why?</li>
    <li>During a take, the level meter touches the top on the loudest chorus. What is the risk, and what do you change for the next take?</li>
    <li>Two mics are recording one acoustic guitar, 15 cm apart, each 10 cm from the strings. Which rule have you broken, and what will you hear?</li>
    <li>Your mix sounds huge in the bedroom and thin in the car. What does that tell you about the monitoring chain?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A condenser for detail — but close-miked, 2–5 cm, with
    the room as dead as duvets can make it: close placement maximises direct sound and the
    room's early reflections are what makes bedrooms boxy. (2) Clipping at the converter —
    distortion written permanently into the file. Lower the preamp gain to restore
    roughly 12–18 dB of headroom at performance level; quiet files lift fine later. (3) The
    3-to-1 rule — spacing should be at least three times the distance to the source, so at
    least 30 cm here. The 5 cm arrival-time difference comb-filters the blend: a hollow,
    phasey tone that EQ cannot remove. (4) The room was lying — untreated walls boosted
    bass in the mix decision. Check mixes on multiple systems and use reference tracks;
    trust the system that keeps being right elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The studio is a chain of translators — source, microphone, preamp, converter — and every link has a noise floor below and a clipping ceiling above.</li>
    <li>Dynamic mics are rugged and forgiving; condensers are detailed and demand +48 V phantom power. Choose for source and room, not price.</li>
    <li>Placement is the first EQ: distance sets the room blend, axis sets tone, and the 3-to-1 rule keeps multi-mic combinations clean.</li>
    <li>Gain staging aims for the middle: far above noise, far below clip — roughly 12–18 dB of digital headroom at performance level.</li>
    <li>The room is on every recording; you can add space later but never remove it. Protect the performance: comfort, whole takes, marked keepers.</li>
    <li>Decisions are only as good as the monitoring behind them — flat monitors, calibrated ears, reference tracks, and cross-checks on other systems.</li>
    <li>Capture, don't rescue: the recording is the production, and mixing can only reshape what was captured.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You can now get a clean, honest recording of what happened in a room. The next lessons
    build the second half of the craft: editing and arranging the takes, mixing them into a
    balance, and the processing toolbox — EQ, compression, reverb — that shapes tone and
    space on purpose rather than by accident. Everything there assumes the foundation here:
    plugins rescue nothing that capture got badly wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Polar patterns</strong> deserve study beyond "directional or not". Cardioid
    (the default) rejects the rear, ideal for isolating a vocal from a room; figure-8 hears
    front and back but rejects the sides — lovely for two singers face-to-face, and the
    basis of mid-side stereo technique; omni hears everything with no proximity effect and
    the most natural low end, and rewards good rooms. Pattern choice is a room strategy as
    much as a timbre choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The conversion stage</strong> has its own fundamentals: sample rate and bit
    depth. CD-standard 44.1 kHz/16-bit captures the audible band, but 24-bit recording buys
    around 144 dB of theoretical dynamic range versus 96 dB — which is what lets modern
    engineers record 18 dB below the ceiling without noise anxiety. Higher sample rates
    (96 kHz) matter mostly for processing headroom and pitch-shifting quality, not
    audibility. <strong>Phantom power</strong> is worth respecting: +48 V down the mic line
    energises condensers, and plugging unbalanced or damaged gear into a phantom-fed input
    can damage it — a classic first-week fault.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, <strong>stereo and ensemble techniques</strong> extend the fundamentals:
    spaced pairs obey the 3-to-1 rule, coincident X/Y pairs trade width for mono
    compatibility, and drums become a system question (how many mics, how much bleed
    between them). Every one of these is still the same three skills in fancier clothes —
    right microphone, right placement, right level.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Session documentation and file discipline</strong> are the fundamentals' quiet
    cousins, and studios run on them. A professional session logs its own anatomy: track
    names that match sources ("Kick-In", "GTR-L-57" rather than Audio-01), sample rate and
    session tempo recorded, mic and preamp settings noted so a recall is possible weeks
    later, and takes marked as they happen. Backups follow the versioned-copy habit — the
    session duplicated with a dated suffix at every significant milestone, one copy off the
    machine — because no mix has ever been saved by a corrupted hard drive. The reasoning is
    the same as the gain staging: the recording exists to be used later, by people
    (including future-you) who were not there. Notes that take two minutes per song save
    hours of guessing per album, and the engineer's reputation for recallable, well-named,
    backed-up sessions is worth as much as their ears.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Edexcel A Level Music Technology specification; Huber &amp; Runstein, <em>Modern Recording Techniques</em>; Sound on Sound recording guides. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

</TopicPage>
